WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.520
There’s an urban legend that dialling 999
--

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.320
the British equivalent of 911 or 112 --

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.460
there's an urban legend that dialling that
number

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.910
will charge your phone battery.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.480
And if you look online, you’ll see a couple
of police forces in the UK

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.240
put out press releases saying it’s not true,

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.260
don’t do it, it ties up an emergency line.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.890
And there are articles and comment threads
tearing into the people who believe this.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.570
How can they be so stupid?

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.070
What idiot would believe this sort of thing?

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.029
Except the weird thing is, it’s almost true.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:32.239
Do you remember Blackberries? They were those
phones that businesspeople had

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:34.309
before actual smartphones came along.

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:38.510
Their UK headquarters is behind me, here in
Slough.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.960
[Despairing] Slough.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.639
Back when Blackberry did have a market share
above 1%,

00:00:44.639 --> 00:00:48.539
it wasn't just business people who used their
phones: teenagers did too.

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:51.999
BBM – BlackBerry Messenger – was the WhatsApp
of its day.

00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:54.559
Instant, free, unlimited text messaging,

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:56.670
as long as everyone you knew had a Blackberry
phone –-

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.399
and as long as that phone had battery power.

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.510
See, when certain types of BlackBerries were
really low on battery,

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.710
they’d go into a locked-down “emergency
calls only” mode.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:06.810
No BBM. No anything.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.439
You weren’t getting any use out of it.

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:11.549
It just said “battery too low for radio
use”.

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:13.170
It'd let you call the emergency services,

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.859
just in case, but nothing else.

00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:18.390
That was a fairly sensible idea by the BlackBerry
engineers.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.270
But they went about it in a really half-assed
way,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.880
and they didn’t think what people would
actually want to do with their phones.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.229
Because there are emergencies that don’t
involve calling 999.

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:28.659
Being lost or alone or desperate,

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:31.359
and just needing to reach one person.

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:34.659
[sad music] Mum. I'm sorry.

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:36.340
I've made a big mistake.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.450
I went to Slough.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.899
It turned out that on a Blackberry,

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:44.149
if you made an emergency call and then hung
up immediately...

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.499
the radios in the phone stayed on. The phone
stayed unlocked.

00:01:48.499 --> 00:01:51.329
With all its connections intact. It’d last
a good few minutes,

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:53.710
as it drained the last bits of its power.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.109
Except if you locked it, or the screen turned
off.

00:01:56.109 --> 00:01:57.350
Then, depending on the model,

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.679
you might have to dial 999 again to unlock
it.

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:02.479
You can see where the myth came from.

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:04.770
And mobile phone providers in the UK,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.420
they get charged somewhere around a pound
every time

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.299
someone calls 999 through them, even for an
instant,

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:12.239
so they weren’t too happy about this either.

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:16.110
Now, I'm not saying there isn’t a space
for careless code in the world,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.799
for quickly developed ideas.

00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:19.890
Heaven knows I've made enough of them.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.170
But start cutting corners in a phone that's
used by millions of --

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.190
was used by millions of people,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.260
and suddenly you’ve got enough hoax calls
coming in

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.180
that it starts to block actual emergencies.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.220
Tiny decisions that end up making a big difference.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.970
I'm going to get out of Slough now.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.900
